Remi macOS “Liquid Glass” Modernization Plan

Goals
- Adopt Apple’s modern glass aesthetic across popovers, windows, and key surfaces.
- Keep strong readability and performance while supporting older macOS with graceful fallbacks.

Target OS & APIs
- Primary: macOS 15+ (Sequoia) with SwiftUI `View.glassBackgroundEffect(in:)` and Materials.
- Fallback: macOS 12–14 using SwiftUI `Material` (e.g., `.ultraThinMaterial`, `.regularMaterial`) or AppKit `NSVisualEffectView` via `NSViewRepresentable`.

High‑Level Changes
1) Popover Container (main entry)
   - Wrap the popover’s root `ContentView` background with `glassBackgroundEffect(in: .roundedRectangle(cornerRadius: 14))`.
   - Hide scroll backgrounds inside to avoid clashing backgrounds: `.scrollContentBackground(.hidden)`.
   - Add subtle border + separator overlays for depth.

2) Cards & Panels (Nooks, Editor, Quick Actions)
   - Introduce a reusable `GlassCard` view (or modifier) that:
     - Applies `glassBackgroundEffect(in:)` on macOS 15+.
     - Falls back to `.background(.ultraThinMaterial, in: RoundedRectangle(cornerRadius: 12))` on older macOS.
     - Adds a 1px stroke and a faint top highlight.
   - Replace existing solid backgrounds in `NookListView`, `TaskEditorView`, Quick Actions panel with `GlassCard`.

3) Headers & Tool Areas
   - For custom headers/tool rows, use a slim glass strip (rounded rectangle or capsule as appropriate) with reduced opacity.
   - Prefer content tints (accent-like) using `foregroundStyle(.secondary)` where needed so text remains legible on glass.

4) Settings Window
   - Root stack gets a large rounded glass background.
   - We already use `.windowStyle(.hiddenTitleBar)`; ensure the content has proper top padding to breathe under the title area.

5) Theme Update (light/dark harmony)
   - Add a small set of tints in `Theme.swift` for glass overlays:
     - Glass tint light: white → clear gradient at 3–6% opacity.
     - Glass tint dark: black → clear gradient at 5–8% opacity.
   - Use these for strokes/highlights to avoid pure white/black halos.

6) Performance & Accessibility
   - Avoid deeply nested glass effects; prefer one parent glass layer with simple children.
   - Test blur/readability with large type sizes and Increase Contrast.
   - Prefer shape-clipped glass to reduce offscreen blur cost.

Implementation Notes (Code Sketches)
- Popover background (macOS 15+):
  ZStack {
    // content
  }
  .background {
    if #available(macOS 15, *) {
      Color.clear
        .glassBackgroundEffect(in: .roundedRectangle(cornerRadius: 14))
        .overlay(
          RoundedRectangle(cornerRadius: 14)
            .strokeBorder(LinearGradient(colors: [Color.white.opacity(0.15), Color.white.opacity(0.02)], startPoint: .top, endPoint: .bottom), lineWidth: 1)
        )
        .overlay(
          Divider().opacity(0.06), alignment: .top
        )
    } else {
      RoundedRectangle(cornerRadius: 14)
        .fill(.ultraThinMaterial)
        .overlay(
          RoundedRectangle(cornerRadius: 14)
            .strokeBorder(Color.white.opacity(0.12), lineWidth: 1)
        )
    }
  }
  .scrollContentBackground(.hidden)

- Reusable card:
  struct GlassCard<Content: View>: View {
    let corner: CGFloat
    @ViewBuilder var content: Content
    var body: some View {
      content
        .padding(12)
        .background {
          if #available(macOS 15, *) {
            Color.clear.glassBackgroundEffect(in: .roundedRectangle(cornerRadius: corner))
          } else {
            RoundedRectangle(cornerRadius: corner).fill(.ultraThinMaterial)
          }
        }
        .overlay(
          RoundedRectangle(cornerRadius: corner)
            .strokeBorder(Color.white.opacity(0.10), lineWidth: 1)
        )
    }
  }

Integration Plan (Concrete Steps)
1) Add `UI/Components/Reusable/GlassCard.swift` with the reusable glass component.
2) Update popover root (`ContentView`) to apply glass background and hide scroll backgrounds.
3) Replace legacy solid backgrounds in:
   - `UI/Views/NookListView.swift` (list container or each nook card)
   - `UI/Views/TaskEditorView.swift` (editor panel)
   - `UI/Views/OnboardingView.swift` (primary panels)
   - AI Quick Actions panel container
4) Add a small `Theme/Glass.swift` (optional) with tint colors and gradients.
5) Verify text/icon contrast; tweak foregroundStyle and opacities as needed.
6) Gate the new effect with availability checks; keep older macOS looking consistent using Materials.

QA Checklist
- Text remains legible over busy wallpapers (light/dark, increased contrast on).
- Popover edges: no double borders; separators align to pixel grid.
- Scrolling doesn’t reveal opaque backgrounds.
- CPU/GPU stable when resizing or showing popover repeatedly.

References
- SwiftUI `glassBackgroundEffect(in:)` (macOS 15+)
  https://developer.apple.com/documentation/swiftui/view/glassbackgroundeffect
- SwiftUI `Material` and HIG Materials
  https://developer.apple.com/documentation/swiftui/material
  https://developer.apple.com/design/human-interface-guidelines/macos/visual-design/materials
- AppKit `NSVisualEffectView` (fallback and advanced control)
  https://developer.apple.com/documentation/appkit/nsvisualeffectview
  https://developer.apple.com/documentation/appkit/nsvisualeffectview/material
